ap in the sun by the lake with the kids was healing. Xavier Chen: You filmed little clips of the campfire, the cannonball contest, and grandma laughing, and you are cutting them into a short movie. Yara Aziz: The reunion fell at the end of Ramadan, so you broke your last few fasts at the lake and explained it to the little cousins over dinner. Zach Morrison: The quiet cabin porch was where you retreated when the crowd got to be too much. Stepping away and coming back was its own kind of belonging. Amira Khalil: Money is tight at home, and for one weekend at the lake none of that followed you. You laughed so hard during the three-legged race that you forgot to worry. Brandon Lee: You tend to keep to yourself at big gatherings. Your cousin handed you a fishing rod and sat with you in silence for an hour. Chloe Park: You brought two rescue dogs from the shelter, and the family fell in love with a scruffy pit bull named Rusty. Dante Romano: Big loud group meals are the hardest part, so you ate breakfast early on the quiet porch. Grandma joined you for those sunrise breakfasts. Elena Vasquez: Your grandma tells stories about crossing the border, and hearing her tell them again by the fire showed how much of this family she carried here. Finn Murphy: Last fall was rough and you almost skipped the reunion out of pride. Nobody asked why your life looked different. They made space for you at the lake. Gia Washington: Big group toasts make your chest tight, so you wrote everyone a little note and left them on the pillows. Holden Price: Your grandfather showed you the little workshop, and you fixed the dock railing together without a single nail. Isla Fernandez: You learned three ukulele chords over the winter and played at the campfire. It was shaky, and the little cousins sang along. Jasper Cole: You are not much of a writer, so you dictated most of this walking the lake trail and then cleaned it up. It sounds honest, like the weekend did.
